    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Prove $\\dfrac{1}{1-\\cos\\alpha}+\\dfrac{1}{1+\\cos\\alpha}=2\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\alpha$, where $\\sin\\alpha\\ne0$.",
    "solution_text": "$\\begin{align*}\\mathrm{LHS}&=\\frac{1}{1-\\cos\\alpha}+\\frac{1}{1+\\cos\\alpha}\\\\&=\\frac{(1+\\cos\\alpha)+(1-\\cos\\alpha)}{(1-\\cos\\alpha)(1+\\cos\\alpha)}\\\\&=\\frac{2}{1-\\cos^2\\alpha}\\\\&=\\frac{2}{\\sin^2\\alpha}\\\\&=2\\operatorname{cosec}^2\\alpha\\\\&=\\mathrm{RHS}.\\end{align*}$",
    "equationAlignment": {
      "mode": "continuation"
    }
  },
  {
    "question_text": "Simplify $3(\\sec\\beta-\\tan\\beta)(\\sec\\beta+\\tan\\beta)+2(\\operatorname{cosec}\\beta-\\cot\\beta)(\\operatorname{cosec}\\beta+\\cot\\beta)$, where $\\sin\\beta\\ne0$ and $\\cos\\beta\\ne0$.",
